  "notes": "West leads the ♣8 against 6NT. South has 11 top tricks (the ♠A, 3 each in hearts and diamonds, and 4 clubs). South must look towards spades for trick number 12.\n\nAfter winning the opening lead, South should lead a spade intending to play the ♠9 from dummy if West follows low. Hopefully, West will have the ♠10 and at least 1 of the ♠K and ♠Q. On the given lie of the cards East wins the ♠K.\n\nSouth wins the return in hand. He leads another spade, playing the ♠J when West follows low. If all goes according to plan, the ♠J will hold and South will have 12 tricks.\n\nThe alternative plan, leading a low spade to the ♠J will work only if West has both the ♠K and ♠Q. This plan is inferior to the recommended line as it requires West to have 2 specific cards (the ♠K and ♠Q). The recommended line only requires West having 1 specific card (the ♠10) and at least 1 of 2 other cards (the ♠K and ♠Q).",
